      "problem": "Consider the function $F(x)=|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{\\pi (4{n}^{2}-1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{4{n}^{2}-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\pi  & =2-4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{4{n}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2-\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{4{n}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
